{"type": "message", "id": "d007991a", "parentId": "0141e581", "timestamp": "2026-04-12T12:42:30.894Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_ss8qfk8w0lbvpsa9xq94fbpo", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"arXiv cs 2020 figure 7 Attribution IoU\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 2272,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2891584c94efdcab\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nTowards Rigorous Interpretations: a Formalisation of Feature Attribution\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2891584c94efdcab\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.12437\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"38a94788224b512e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMore generated examples are given in ﬁgures 7 and 8, as ... D. Experimental details · We list more implementation details, conﬁgurations and ... GAM, ... GA∞M · No hyper-parameter. These methods directly use the condi- Towards Rigorous Interpretations: a Formalisation of Feature Attribution\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"38a94788224b512e\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8fcb9233f9b52872\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUnified-IoU: For High-Quality Object Detection\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8fcb9233f9b52872\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2408.06636v1\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4bf5ccc2d2fba8eb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(2020). In this way, the trained model will produce large bounding box regression errors in target detection, especially when facing dense datasets, and it is easy to produce a lot of missing and false detection. Different studies have different definitions of anchor box quality. It is common to use center distance or IoU value to define the quality of anchor box and weight factor, but sometimes this is wrong, as shown in Figure ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4bf5ccc2d2fba8eb\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"August 13, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"327da24a44aa2e1a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[1911.08287] Distance-IoU Loss: Faster and Better Learning for Bounding Box Regression\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"327da24a44aa2e1a\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.08287\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9a2cb1dd264cab7f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAbstract:Bounding box regression is the crucial step in object detection. In existing methods, while $\\\\ell_n$-norm loss is widely adopted for bounding box regression, it is not tailored to the evaluation metric, i.e., Intersection over Union (IoU). Recently, IoU loss and generalized IoU (GIoU) loss have been proposed to benefit the IoU metric, but still suffer from the problems of slow convergence and inaccurate regression.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9a2cb1dd264cab7f\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"November 19, 2019\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"11c627bbd8adcd34\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPublished as a conference paper at ICLR 2020 ON IDENTIFIABILITY IN TRANSFORMERS\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"11c627bbd8adcd34\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1908.04211\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e9e8a1ca16ce3d5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn the ﬁgures, <strong>blue corresponds to “previous” tokens and red to “next”</strong> ... bour lookup. The identiﬁability rate drops both with increasing k, but also with increasing depth. Interestingly, recovering left (blue) and right (red) neighbours shows different behaviour, indicat- ing ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e9e8a1ca16ce3d5\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c5ede17a601825a5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n1 Introduction\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c5ede17a601825a5\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.09461v1\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"776cd8af587fed54\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe goal is to determine which architecture can handle the extremely subtle, thin, low-contrast CS structures. RF-DETR provides the strongest detection performance by a large margin. While all YOLO variants struggle to exceed mAP@50 values above 26% and show limited gains even at the more permissive mAP@25 threshold, the RT-DETR models reach 60–80% at IoU=0.5 and 70–88% at IoU=0.25. However, RF-DETR substantially surpasses all of them. The RF-DETR-m variant achieves a leading performance of 93.1 mAP@25, 45.7 mAP, 89.5 mAP@50, and 39.7 mAP@75, establishing a clear SOTA for CS localization.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"776cd8af587fed54\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 10, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0a20fb85c5453c3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCharacterizing soot in TEM images using a convolutional neural network\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0a20fb85c5453c3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.09272\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"464d386c828dfb3e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor semantic segmentation, a second common measure is the intersection-over-union (IoU).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"464d386c828dfb3e\\\"