{"type": "message", "id": "95f1915b", "parentId": "848fdfe6", "timestamp": "2026-04-12T12:43:41.685Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Wait, this isn't working. Let's go back to the original problem. The user says: \"Which method with median Attribution IoU lower than 0.7 shows the least variability?\" \n\nWait, I remember that in the standard Attribution IoU box plots from XAI papers, the methods with median <0.7 are things like Vanilla Backprop, Guided Backprop, InputXGradient, Integrated Gradients (maybe?), and the one with least variability is usually **Guided Backprop**? Or wait, no—wait, Grad-CAM has median ~0.7, so it's excluded. Wait, SmoothGrad has higher variability? No, wait, let's think: the method with the smallest box (IQR) among those with median <0.7. \n\nWait, another angle: let's look for \"Attribution IoU box plot least variability\". Let's try that search.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_cpidrsjryuocg83b6c1qiavg", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "Attribution IoU box plot least variability", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "volcengine", "model": "doubao-seed-2-0-mini-260215", "usage": {"input": 3691, "output": 418, "cacheRead": 36664, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 40773, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775997817668, "responseId": "021775997817249f9065cef9012c43073e2d0226029aed4e8bbdb"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "84f27f5c", "parentId": "95f1915b", "timestamp": "2026-04-12T12:43:44.089Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_cpidrsjryuocg83b6c1qiavg", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"Attribution IoU box plot least variability\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 2402,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"db2157838e74c1a5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHow to Interpret Variability in Box Plots\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"db2157838e74c1a5\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.statology.org/box-plot-variability/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3e119cb8260aad1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nConversely, we can see that <strong>Team C has the least variation in points scored because their box plot has the least distance between the two ends of the box</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3e119cb8260aad1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 19, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.statology.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8313283c42572cd3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFull article: Developing initial notions of variability when learning about box plots\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8313283c42572cd3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10986065.2024.2421412\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1a09cb27d7043a01\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn conclusion, we operationalized a correct understanding of the box area – which is considered as an important part of conceptual knowledge on the box plot representation – by a reduced likelihood of exhibiting the area bias. Both, item types were modeled each as a latent variable (reading-off descriptive parameters, four items, McDonald’s ω = 0.80 and misinterpretation of the box area, five items, McDonald’s ω = 0.91).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1a09cb27d7043a01\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.tandfonline.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bfd4911b0ee1e7b7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUsing Box Plots to Understand Variability and Outliers in Data\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bfd4911b0ee1e7b7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sixsigmadsi.com/box-plots/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3c949e367900e6d7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBox plots allow you to visually compare the variation among... Learn how to make Box Plots with labels in excel with these examples...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3c949e367900e6d7\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 2, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sixsigmadsi.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f086418fc09dfb4e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nInterquartile Range and Boxplots (2 of 3) | Statistics for the Social Sciences\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f086418fc09dfb4e\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://courses.lumenlearning.com/atd-herkimer-statisticssocsci/chapter/interquartile-range-and-boxplots-2-of-3/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6a2127240b4c9742\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNotice: A long box in the boxplot indicates a large IQR, so the middle half of the data has a lot of variability. A short box in the boxplot indicates a small IQR.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6a2127240b4c9742\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"courses.lumenlearning.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d56206cdbc81eb79\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBox plot - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d56206cdbc81eb79\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box_plot\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"678f28782639e146\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn descriptive statistics, a box plot or boxplot is a method for demonstrating graphically the locality, spread and skewness groups of numerical data through their quartiles. In addition to the box on a box plot, there can be lines (which are called whiskers) extending from the box indicating ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"678f28782639e146\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6e45156937275a8f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nvariance - What is the defintion of variation in a box plot? - Cross Validated\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6e45156937275a8f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/488224/what-is-the-defintion-of-variation-in-a-box-plot\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f8ce04a072e135d5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEach boxplot depicts at least seven quantities, as labeled in the Thursday boxplot (although some may coincide): <strong>eXtremes, Fences (the tips of the whiskers), Hinges (the borders of the boxes), and the Median</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f8ce04a072e135d5\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"September 19, 2020\",\n      \"siteName\": \"stats.stackexchange.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"867d3722c6541b40\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBox Plots | Introduction to Statistics\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"867d3722c6541b40\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://courses.lumenlearning.com/introstats1/chapter/box-plots/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3413504d839a5f5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBox plots (also called box-and-whisker plots or box-whisker plots) give a good graphical image of the concentration of the data. They also show how far the extreme values are from most of the data. A box plot is constructed from five values: the minimum value, the first quartile, the median, ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3413504d839a5f5\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"courses.lumenlearning.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2e7ddaa6c4511465\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBox Plot | Introduction to Statistics\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2e7ddaa6c4511465\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.jmp.com/en/statistics-knowledge-portal/exploratory-data-analysis/box-plot\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0bfce5ff742c2de7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA box plot shows the distribution of data for a continuous variable · Box plots help you see the center and spread of data. You can also use them as a visual tool to check for normality or to identify points that may be outliers\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0bfce5ff742c2de7\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.jmp.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0d9685d27ff04824\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n1.3.3.7. Box Plot - Information Technology Laboratory\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0d9685d27ff04824\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/boxplot.htm\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f7300ca2a94e7116\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis box plot, comparing four machines for energy output, shows that machine has a significant effect on energy with respect to both location and variation. Machine 3 has the highest energy response (about 72.5); <strong>machine 4 has the least variable energy response 